# Medicare Healthcare Fraud Detection Using Machine Learning
## Comprehensive Summary: Sections 1 - 11.1

---

## Executive Summary

This project develops a machine learning-based system to detect fraudulent healthcare providers in Medicare Part B and Part D programs. By integrating Medicare claims data (2023) with the Office of Inspector General (OIG) exclusion list, we created a labeled dataset of 1,572,829 providers with 270 known fraudulent cases (0.0172% fraud rate). Through systematic feature engineering and statistical feature selection, we reduced the dimensionality from 42 to 21 features, achieving a 36.4% reduction while maintaining predictive power. The analysis employed multiple machine learning models, with Random Forest achieving promising results in identifying fraudulent providers.

**Key Achievements:**
- Processed and merged 3 large-scale datasets totaling over 30 million records
- Engineered 10 domain-specific features capturing billing patterns and service diversity
- Applied rigorous statistical feature selection reducing multicollinearity and noise
- Balanced extreme class imbalance (5824:1 ratio) using SMOTE
- Trained baseline and advanced models demonstrating fraud detection capability

