## Section 9: Data Splitting & Scaling

### 9.1 Train-Test Split

**Objective:** Prepare data for model training using the 21 selected features from Section 8.

**Feature Matrix (X):**
- Features: 21 selected from statistical analysis
- Samples: 1,572,829 providers
- Shape: (1,572,829, 21)

**Target Vector (y):**
- Binary label: Is_Fraudulent (0 or 1)
- Shape: (1,572,829,)
- Class distribution:
  - Non-Fraudulent (0): 1,572,559 (99.9828%)
  - Fraudulent (1): 270 (0.0172%)

**Split Configuration:**
- **Test Size:** 20% (314,566 samples)
- **Train Size:** 80% (1,258,263 samples)
- **Stratification:** Yes (maintains 0.0172% fraud rate in both sets)
- **Random State:** 42 (reproducibility)

**Split Results:**
- Training set: (1,258,263, 21)
  - Fraud rate: 0.0172%
  - Fraudulent cases: 216
- Test set: (314,566, 21)
  - Fraud rate: 0.0172%
  - Fraudulent cases: 54

**Categorical Information Preserved:**
- NPI, Provider_Name, State, Zipcode, Provider_Type, Entity_Code, Data_Source
- Stored separately for later analysis/interpretation

### 9.2 Handle Class Imbalance

**Challenge:** Extreme class imbalance (5824:1 ratio) will cause models to predict all 0s (non-fraud).

**Solution:** SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique)

**Configuration:**
- Random state: 42
- k_neighbors: 5 (default)
- Applied to training set only (prevents data leakage)

**Before SMOTE:**
- Class 0 (Non-Fraud): 1,258,047
- Class 1 (Fraud): 216
- Imbalance ratio: 5824.29:1

**After SMOTE:**
- Class 0 (Non-Fraud): 1,258,047
- Class 1 (Fraud): 1,258,047 (synthetic samples generated)
- Imbalance ratio: 1.00:1 (perfectly balanced)

**New Training Set:**
- Shape: (2,516,094, 21)
- Doubled size due to synthetic fraud samples

**Important:** Test set remains unchanged (original distribution) to evaluate real-world performance.

### 9.3 Feature Scaling

**Method:** RobustScaler (resistant to outliers)

**Rationale:**
- Healthcare data contains outliers (e.g., exceptionally high charges, rare cases)
- RobustScaler uses median and IQR instead of mean and standard deviation
- Better suited than StandardScaler for skewed distributions

**Scaling Process:**
1. Fit scaler on training data (learns median and IQR)
2. Transform training data
3. Transform test data (using training statistics - no data leakage)

**Results:**
- Training data scaled: (2,516,094, 21)
- Test data scaled: (314,566, 21)
- All features normalized to comparable ranges

**Data Ready for Model Training:**
- ✓ 21 statistically selected features
- ✓ Balanced training set (1:1 ratio)
- ✓ Scaled features (robust to outliers)
- ✓ Untouched test set (realistic evaluation)

